    <<link "Continue" "R1_Martin_Hallway">><</link>>
</div>

:: R1_Martin_Hallway [r1 story]
<<location "Hallway, Floor 39">>
<<objective "Prepare for the Henderson meeting on Thursday.">>
<<portrait "files/images/characters/martin_corner_office.png" "Martin Crane">>

Martin Crane intercepts me in the hallway at 11:40, which is Martin Crane's way of saying he was waiting for me to walk by. He's fifty-five, six-foot-one, built like a man who played football in college and never entirely stopped believing the world owed him for it. Silver hair swept back. Suit that costs more than my monthly car payment. Handshake like he's testing whether your bones are load-bearing.

<<say "Martin Crane">>Ashford.<</say>>

He grips my hand. I grip back, the way my father taught me: firm, two seconds, eye contact, release.

<<say "Martin Crane">>Henderson account ready for Thursday?<</say>>

<<sayself>>Yes, sir. Three-scenario breakdown, full competitive analysis, updated projections.<</sayself>>

<<say "Martin Crane">>Good.<</say>>

He claps my shoulder. The impact is somewhere between affection and establishing dominance, a gesture he probably picked up from his own father, or from a book about leadership with a wolf on the cover.

<<say "Martin Crane">>You've been solid on this one. Keep it tight.<</say>>

He starts to walk away. Big strides. The hallway parts for him without him noticing because he's never had to notice.

I stand there for a moment. My shoulder warm where his hand was. Martin represents something I'm supposed to want. He's the endgame of the architecture my father built: the commanding voice, the corner office, the certainty. I should admire him. I do, technically, in the way you admire a monument you walk past every day. With distance. With the suspicion that up close it's smaller than it looks.

